\description{
Extracts the raster cells around each survey point, records
the distance from the point to each cell, and computes a first set of
kernel-weighted covariates. Fit your starting model to those covariates,
then pass the model and the returned object to \code{\link[=multiScale_optim]{multiScale_optim()}}. This
is the first step of every \code{multiScaleR} workflow, shown in
\code{vignette("v01-quickstart", package = "multiScaleR")}.
}
\section{Prepare the points and rasters}{
Points and rasters must share a defined, projected CRS. Every distance,
including \code{max_D} and any \code{sigma} you supply, is in the linear unit of
that projection, usually meters. Landscape and surface metrics also need
square cells. Row names of \code{pts} are kept throughout, so model data can be
joined back to the points.

Each point's buffer is the circle of radius \code{max_D}, and every cell it
intersects is extracted, including partly covered boundary cells, which
keep their full kernel weight. Missing cells drop out of each weighted
mean, and the remaining weights renormalize. Extraction runs in batches of
25 points to limit the peak memory of temporary tables. Point order,
returned values, and distances are not affected.
}

\section{Manage memory}{
A larger \code{max_D} extracts more cells per point. Check the size of the
object and the memory needed for parallel optimization with
\code{\link[=estimate_multiscale_ram]{estimate_multiscale_ram()}}. When every covariate is a kernel mean,
\code{store_cell_data = FALSE} keeps only the bins.
}

\section{Related preparation functions}{
To assign each observation to a different annual raster while estimating
one pooled landscape relationship, use \code{\link[=kernel_prep_by_group]{kernel_prep_by_group()}}. To
prepare inputs for \code{unmarked} models, see \code{\link[=prepare_unmarked_data]{prepare_unmarked_data()}}.
}

